WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Deadlines and cancellation are security-relevant resource controls, not only reliability features.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:12.200
A deadline tells the service how long the client is willing to wait.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.820
Cancellation tells the service the call no longer needs to continue.

4
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.240
Together, they help bound work and keep resources from being consumed after the result is no longer useful.

5
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.360
Clients should communicate realistic deadlines based on the operation.

6
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:34.620
A fast lookup, report generation request, stream subscription, and cross-service workflow may each need different expectations.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:45.440
Without deadlines, slow dependencies and partial failures can tie up threads, connections, memory, and downstream capacity longer than intended.

8
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.320
Services should honor cancellation where it is safe to do so.

9
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:58.860
If the caller has gone away or the deadline has passed, continuing expensive work may waste resources and distort operational signals.

10
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:06.640
Cancellation handling should be built into service methods, downstream calls, and long-running loops in a way that preserves data consistency.

11
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.440
Deadline and cancellation propagation matter when one gRPC service calls another service.

12
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.380
A parent call that is nearly out of time should not start a child call as if it had a full budget.

13
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:26.160
Propagating intent helps the system fail predictably and prevents one slow path from consuming capacity across multiple services.

14
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:34.680
Retries can improve resilience for transient failures, but careless retries can amplify load or repeat work that was not safe to repeat.

15
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:42.140
Retried operations should be reviewed for idempotency, side effects, backend capacity, and user expectations.

16
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.040
A read call, a state change, and a payment-like workflow should not be treated the same.

17
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:56.280
The reliability baseline is defensive: define deadlines, honor cancellation, propagate time budgets, review retry policies, and monitor outcomes.

18
00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:01.500
These choices help the service remain predictable when dependencies slow down or clients disconnect.
